 event rates: a binomial approach</article-title><source>Controlled Clinical Trials</source><year>1993</year><volume>14</volume><fpage>198</fpage><lpage>208</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0197-2456(93)90003-V</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8339550</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B19"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Flather</surname><given-names>MD</given-names></name><name><surname>Farkouh</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name><name><surname>Pogue</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Yusuf</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><article-title>Strengths and limitations of meta-analysis: larger studies may be more reliable</article-title><source>Control ClinTrials</source><year>1977</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>568</fpage><lpage>579</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0197-2456(97)00024-X</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B20"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Gavaghan</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Tram&#x000e8;r</surname><given-names>MR</given-names></name><name><surname>Collins</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name><name><surname>McQuay</surname><given-names>HJ</given-names></name><article-title>Size is everything&#x02013;large amounts of information are needed to overcome random effects in estimating direction and magnitude of treatment effects</article-title><source>Pain</source><year>1998</year><volume>78</volume><fpage>209</fpage><lpage>216</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0304-3959(98)00140-7</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9870574</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>